{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:47:31.712Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"34","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"CONFIRMED and FIXED in feb63a86. The premise is exactly right, including the mechanism.\n\nREPRODUCED FIRST, before touching anything. Input = one resolvable diagnostic, one 5 MiB line,\none more resolvable diagnostic:\n\n    build := \"./a.go:1:1: undefined: Alpha\\n\" + strings.Repeat(\"x\", 5*1024*1024) + \"\\n./b.go:2:2: undefined: Beta\\n\"\n    candidates, scanned, err := explainCandidates(strings.NewReader(build), 8)\n\n    candidates=[Alpha] scanned=1 err=bufio.Scanner: token too long\n\nBeta is gone. bufio.Scanner returns ErrTooLong at that token and Scan() then returns false\nforever, so everything after the long line was never looked at. The drain that follows\n(io.Copy(io.Discard, source)) does keep the echo whole -- the agent still SEES those lines --\nwhich is precisely what made this quiet: the output looked complete while explain resolved\nnothing from half of it. Tolerating ErrTooLong at explain.go:241 stopped the command failing; it\ndid not restore the scan.\n\nFIX: read lines from a bufio.Reader rather than a Scanner. New helper explainReadLine appends\nat most explainMaxLineBytes (4 MiB, the ceiling the Scanner was already configured with, so no\nline that used to be scanned is scanned less) and discards the remainder of an over-long line by\nlooping on bufio.ErrBufferFull, so the next call starts on the NEXT line. ErrTooLong can no\nlonger occur, so the special case at the call site is gone rather than left as a decoy.\n\nREGRESSION TESTS, both in internal/cli/explain_context_test.go. On a compile-clean revert --\nexplainCandidates put back on bufio.NewScanner with the same buffer bounds and the ErrTooLong\ntolerance restored; go build ./... and go vet ./internal/cli/ both clean, \"REVERT COMPILES\nCLEAN\" -- both fail:\n\n    TestExplainKeepsScanningPastAnOverLongLine\n      explain_context_test.go:252: names = [Alpha], want [Alpha Beta]: the diagnostic after the over-long line was dropped\n\n    TestExplainTruncatesAnOverLongLineRatherThanJoiningIt\n      explain_context_test.go:276: candidates = [], want only Gamma: text past the per-line cap is not scanned, and the next line must still be\n\nThe second one is the guard on the fix rather than on the bug: it plants a name PAST the per-line\ncap and a different name on the following line, and pins that the capped text is not scanned, the\nfollowing line still is, and the long line's bytes do not leak into the next line's file context\n(it asserts Gamma resolves with file c.go).\n\nMEMORY UNCHANGED, which was the property the Scanner's buffer bound existed to protect and the\none a naive \"just read the whole line\" fix would have destroyed. Measured with runtime.MemStats\nacross explainCandidates on a 300 MB single line sandwiched between two diagnostics:\n\n    candidates=2 err=\u003cnil\u003e  heapAllocDelta=8 MB  totalAlloc=23 MB\n\nBoth diagnostics resolved, 8 MB of heap for a 300 MB line. ReadSlice never allocates past the\nreader's own 64 KiB buffer, and at most 4 MiB of any single line is retained.\n\nVERIFICATION: go build ./... clean; go vet ./internal/cli/ clean; gofmt clean; go test\n./internal/cli/ -count=1 passes in full (36.2s), which includes the two tests added earlier this\nround for the --max-symbols budget.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1S6VZV1HS0F1TFSCPGK4HQR","targetId":"01M1S6VZVEXZFP4XETAS2WKSX7","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NGN3YVFB3SMZCH1CBV49WV"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
