{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:55:41.998Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"39","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"DUPLICATE of 01M1P6VKT20RH850K1JE3JRSRB, same anchor (internal/cli/help.go:250), dismissed\nearlier this round with measurements. Routed to design issue #215\n(https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/issues/215), which is open. Re-posting it does not\nchange the disposition, but this version adds two things, so both are answered here and appended\nto #215 rather than dropped.\n\nWHAT WAS ALREADY SETTLED, briefly, so nobody re-derives it:\n\n  \"defeating the new streaming behavior\" -- REFUTED, measured. explain's own peak RSS is\n  217,759,744 B at 10 MB of input and 219,856,896 B at 100 MB: +2.1 MB for +90 MB. The streaming\n  fix does exactly what it was written to do. The buffer is in the SHELL, and it did not come\n  back into explain.\n\n  \"can again consume unbounded memory\" -- CONFIRMED and larger than stated. Same 100 MB:\n  `cat f | cat` peaks at 2,146,304 B, `o=$(cat f); printf '%s\\n' \"$o\"` at 604,733,440 B. 281x,\n  scaling with output size.\n\n  Not fixed here because the plain-pipe revert reopens the defect this PR closed -- verified,\n  `/bin/sh -c \"false 2\u003e\u00261 | entire graph explain ...\"` exits 0, the capture exits 1 -- and\n  because the shape is a documented product surface at four sites, one of which (the emitted\n  VERIFY: line, `prefix = \"( o=$(\"` in internal/sem/search_verify.go) is on the unmerged branch\n  fix/verify-exit-codes-and-injection. Changing help.go alone would make three shapes where\n  there are two.\n\nNEW IN THIS VERSION, and both accepted:\n\n1. \"emits no incremental output\" -- CONFIRMED, and it is the strongest argument in the finding.\n   Timestamps of lines arriving at the far end, with 3 seconds between the two lines produced:\n\n     plain pipe    1788627290.195 first     capture     1788627296.208 first\n                   1788627293.196 second                1788627296.211 second\n\n   3.0s apart as produced, versus 3ms apart all at the end. On a hung build that is the\n   difference between watching it fail and waiting blind. Added to #215.\n\n2. The suggested remedy -- \"a temporary status file or an explicit portable wrapper\" -- is the\n   right direction, and #215 already carries a verified instance of the second option that is\n   strictly better than the first:\n\n     ( exec 4\u003e\u00261; r=$( { { \u003ccmd\u003e 2\u003e\u00261; echo $? \u003e\u00263; } | entire graph explain --repo . \u003e\u00264; } 3\u003e\u00261 ); exit $r )\n\n   Verified under dash: exit status preserved (rc=7 through the pipeline), 1,523,712 B peak on\n   the same 100 MB input, 397x below the capture form, and it streams. The status-file shape also\n   works but pays for it with a writable TMPDIR, cleanup on signals, and an mktemp failure mode\n   the fd form does not have. Both are now written up in #215.\n\nNote for whoever reads this trail: help.go:250 has now been raised twice and internal/cli/explain.go\ntwice, in four consecutive review rounds. The two explain.go findings were real and are fixed\n(0cc4f28a, feb63a86, each with a regression test that fails on a compile-clean revert). This one\nis the same finding a second time against an unchanged line whose disposition is a maintainer\ndecision. Flagging as thrash and stopping the loop here rather than re-triggering the gate again.","to":"dismissed"},"reviewId":"01M1S7HHDVQF3P0P86PWGTSTEK","targetId":"01M1S7HHEFBYQ4FTG71EGXQ7NW","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NGN3YVFB3SMZCH1CBV49WV"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
