    entire graph explain --repo . < 10MB    217,759,744 B
    entire graph explain --repo . < 100MB   219,856,896 B     <- +2.1 MB for +90 MB of input

If the capture had defeated it, the second number would track the input. It does not. The
buffer moved into the shell; it did not come back into explain.

CLAIM (a) "risking unbounded shell memory" -- CONFIRMED, and larger than the finding implies:

    /bin/sh -c 'cat big100m.txt | cat >/dev/null'                        2,146,304 B
    /bin/sh -c 'o=$(cat big100m.txt); printf "%s\n" "$o" >/dev/null'   604,733,440 B

281x, scaling with output size. Routed to #215 rather than fixed here, for two reasons. First,
the obvious fix is a revert to the pre-#206 plain pipe, and that reopens the defect #206 closed
-- verified, not assumed:

    $ /bin/sh -c "false 2>&1 | entire graph explain --repo . >/dev/null 2>&1"; echo $?
    0        <- test failed, caller told it passed

    $ /bin/sh -c "( o=\$(false 2>&1); r=\$?; printf '%s\n' \"\$o\" | entire graph explain --repo . >/dev/null 2>&1; exit \$r )"; echo $?
    1        <- correct

and the explain.go rejection of `set -o pipefail` is accurate, not folklore:
`/bin/dash -c 'set -o pipefail'` -> "Illegal option -o pipefail", rc=2;
`${PIPESTATUS[0]}` under dash -> "Bad substitution", rc=2.

Second, a POSIX form does exist that streams AND keeps the status, and I verified it works
under dash (this is in #215 so it can just be taken):

    ( exec 4>&1; r=$( { { <cmd> 2>&1; echo $? >&3; } | entire graph explain --repo . >&4; } 3>&1 ); exit $r )

    status: /bin/dash -c '( exec 4>&1; r=$( { { sh -c "echo boom; exit 7" 2>&1; echo $? >&3; } | cat >&4; } 3>&1 ); exit $r )'  -> rc=7
    memory: same shape over big100m.txt -> 1,523,712 B   (397x below the capture form)

    (exec 4>&1 must sit OUTSIDE the substitution; with `3>&1 4>&1` on the inner group both
    descriptors point at the substitution pipe and the build output lands in $r.)

It is 19 characters longer (141 vs 122) and materially harder to adapt -- whoever swaps in
their own test command must not disturb `2>&1; echo $? >&3`, where `o=$(...)` is self-evident.
The comment at explain.go:40 chose the current shape from a measurement (0 agents typed a
two-command pipe across 27 sessions; 79 VERIFY-style commands were run), so trading that
legibility for bounded memory is the shape-owner's call, not a review loop's.

CLAIM (b) "POSIX-only, regresses cross-platform guidance" -- CONFIRMED as a real narrowing, but
it is a product decision, so no syntax was invented here. #206 replaced
`go test ... 2>&1 | entire graph explain --repo .` (valid PowerShell: `2>&1`, `|`, and
`'^TestX$'` all parse) with a line that is not PowerShell at all -- `o=$(...)` is not an
assignment there (PS needs `$o = ...`), `printf` is not a cmdlet, and `$?` is a boolean not a
numeric status. Neither form ever worked in cmd.exe, which does not strip single quotes. And
PowerShell users are genuinely in scope: release.yml:41-46 ships windows/amd64 and
windows/arm64, there are four windows-latest CI jobs, and the release workflow itself uses
`shell: pwsh`. Evidence limit, stated plainly: this is a grammar-level argument, not an
executed reproduction -- there is no pwsh on the host that produced the numbers above; the
Windows release/CI facts are from the workflow files and are directly checkable. No single
string satisfies sh + PowerShell + cmd.exe for an exit-status-preserving pipe, so covering
Windows shells means per-shell emission -- a feature, not a text edit. Question routed to #215.

