{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:47:31.712Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"34","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"CONFIRMED and FIXED in feb63a86. The premise is exactly right, including the mechanism.

REPRODUCED FIRST, before touching anything. Input = one resolvable diagnostic, one 5 MiB line,
one more resolvable diagnostic:

    build := "./a.go:1:1: undefined: Alpha\n" + strings.Repeat("x", 5*1024*1024) + "\n./b.go:2:2: undefined: Beta\n"
    candidates, scanned, err := explainCandidates(strings.NewReader(build), 8)

    candidates=[Alpha] scanned=1 err=bufio.Scanner: token too long

Beta is gone. bufio.Scanner returns ErrTooLong at that token and Scan() then returns false
forever, so everything after the long line was never looked at. The drain that follows
(io.Copy(io.Discard, source)) does keep the echo whole -- the agent still SEES those lines --
which is precisely what made this quiet: the output looked complete while explain resolved
nothing from half of it. Tolerating ErrTooLong at explain.go:241 stopped the command failing; it
did not restore the scan.

FIX: read lines from a bufio.Reader rather than a Scanner. New helper explainReadLine appends
at most explainMaxLineBytes (4 MiB, the ceiling the Scanner was already configured with, so no
line that used to be scanned is scanned less) and discards the remainder of an over-long line by
looping on bufio.ErrBufferFull, so the next call starts on the NEXT line. ErrTooLong can no
longer occur, so the special case at the call site is gone rather than left as a decoy.

REGRESSION TESTS, both in internal/cli/explain_context_test.go. On a compile-clean revert --
