{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:36:05.295Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"27","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Two claims, two verdicts. Fixed in 0cc4f28a.

CLAIM 1 "silently overrides --max-symbols values above 1024" -- CONFIRMED and FIXED.

Reproduced first. `--max-symbols` is parsed by searchNonNegativeIntFlag (explain.go:634), which
accepts any nonnegative int and imposes no ceiling, and the help entry says only "Maximum
distinct symbol names to resolve" with no upper bound. But collection stops the moment the
dedupe set stops growing, so `len(candidates) <= len(seen) <= explainMaxScannedNames`, and
anything above 1024 was answered as 1024 with no signal.

Regression test REDACTED
(internal/cli/explain_context_test.go). On a compile-clean revert -- the three-line raise
removed, `budget` left declared and used so `go build ./...` still succeeds ("REVERT COMPILES
CLEAN") -- it fails:

    explain_context_test.go:201: candidates = 1024, want 1500: --max-symbols above 1024 must not be silently clamped to it

The fix separates the two limits, which answer different questions and only one of which has an
attacker on the other end:

    budget := explainMaxScannedNames
    if limit > budget {
        budget = limit
    }
    ...
    if len(seen) >= budget {

The bound exists so the BUILD cannot choose how much this process remembers; the caller's own
--max-symbols is not the build. Raising it spends nothing new -- `candidates` is already sized
by `limit` regardless of the budget, and an explainCandidate (three strings) is strictly heavier
than a map key -- so the untrusted input still cannot push either structure past a bound the
caller picked. explainMaxScannedNames is now documented as a floor rather than a ceiling.

A second test pins the half that must NOT change:
TestExplainStillBoundsWhatAnUntrustedBuildCanMakeItRemember feeds 5024 distinct names at the
default limit of 8 and asserts scanned == 1024. It passes both before and after, which is the
point -- it is there so a later "simplification" cannot quietly drop the bound while the first
test still goes green.

CLAIM 2 "scanned/omitted underreport inputs containing more than 1024 distinct names" -- TRUE
on the facts, REFUTED as a defect, and its prescribed remedy is impossible as written.

The saturation is real and I have not changed it: at the default limit, 5024 distinct names
still report Scanned 1024 / Omitted 1016. It is also deliberate and documented at the constant
-- "an exact count of it buys nothing worth unbounded memory."

The remedy asked for is "preserve accurate ... count semantics with bounded storage". Those two
cannot both hold. Exact distinct-element counting over a stream requires space linear in the
number of distinct elements; that is the whole reason the cap exists, on input an attacker
controls. Bounded storage buys you saturation or an approximation, never an accurate count. So
the request is not a fix that was skipped, it is unsatisfiable.

What IS a fair residual, and I want it on the record rather than closed silently: at saturation
a consumer cannot tell exactly-1024 from 1024-or-more -- the same ambiguity the explainCandidates
comment says it fixed at the MaxSymbols boundary ("a machine consumer could not tell exactly
eight names from eight out of ninety"), reappearing at the budget boundary. Closing it exactly
is impossible per the above; closing it honestly means a saturation flag on ExplainResponse,
which is a JSON schema addition and so a product call rather than something a review loop should
add unilaterally. Not filed as an issue because nothing observable is wrong today -- Scanned is
already documented as saturating -- but it is the right shape if anyone wants the signal.

VERIFICATION: go build ./... clean; go vet ./internal/cli/ clean; gofmt clean; go test
./internal/cli/ passes in full (39.6s). Branch merged forward from origin/main first and rebuilt
after the merge (3ed19755), since a zero-conflict merge here has broken the build before.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1S62393H6B6FYTVZ19EREJH","targetId":"01M1S6239Q652NXY8MQQ5FXF0C","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NGN3YVFB3SMZCH1CBV49WV"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
